BSI's advisory states only that an attacker can exploit the flaws to run arbitrary code with administrator rights, rates them critical and names pfSense CE 2.7.2 and CE 2.8.0 ([BSI WID-SEC-W-2026-1435](https://wid.cert-bund.de/.well-known/csaf/white/2026/wid-sec-w-2026-1435.json)); it carries no patch status and no mitigation, so it does not mark the CE build as unpatched or recommend a mitigation. CVE-2025-69691 needs an authenticated administrator, not any Basic Auth credentials; the researcher adds that the endpoint is enabled by default and default administrator credentials are widely deployed ([Full Disclosure, 2026-02-16](https://seclists.org/fulldisclosure/2026/Feb/16)). ENISA's EUVD scores CVE-2025-69690 at 9.1, against the researcher's 8.8 ([EUVD-2025-209738](https://euvd.enisa.europa.eu/vulnerability/EUVD-2025-209738)). Netgate's refusal to patch is reported by the researcher ([Full Disclosure, 2026-02-16](https://seclists.org/fulldisclosure/2026/Feb/16)), the CVE records mark both flaws as disputed by the supplier because the functions are admin-only ([EUVD-2025-209739](https://euvd.enisa.europa.eu/vulnerability/EUVD-2025-209739)), and no cited source says whether pfSense Plus is affected. The researcher's post gives no file to audit for persistence, so the check is the firewall's own access logs for pfsense.exec_php calls and unplanned configuration restores.
